    <h1 id="headline">Read the releases.<br><span>Not a dollar signal.</span></h1>
    <p class="dek">The October 26–30 week brought a Federal Reserve decision and new U.S. growth, inflation and labor-cost figures. Here is what the official records establish—and what they cannot tell us about actual market moves.</p>
    <div class="hero-foot">
      <div><strong>Period covered:</strong> 26–30 October 2026</div>
      <div><strong>Edition:</strong> Independent educational Weekly</div>
      <div><strong>Source basis:</strong> Dated official releases</div>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <main id="main" class="container layout">
    <article class="content">
      <div class="mw-ai-disclosure" role="note"><strong>AI-assisted preparation.</strong> AI tools assisted with preparation of this educational review. USD Impact, operated by KELA LEADS S.R.L., is responsible for the published content. The linked Federal Reserve, BEA and BLS releases are the sources for reported official facts.</div>
      <section aria-labelledby="summary"><div class="section-kicker">01 / Executive summary</div><h2 id="summary">Four releases, one important distinction</h2>
        <p>The FOMC held its target range at <strong>3.75%–4.00%</strong> on October 28. A day later, the Bureau of Economic Analysis released third-quarter GDP and September personal income and outlays data. On October 30, the Bureau of Labor Statistics reported third-quarter employment costs.</p>
        <p>These official observations help frame how monetary policy, economic activity and inflation can influence expectations for the U.S. dollar. They <strong>do not establish</strong> whether DXY, Treasury yields, WTI, gas, gold, Bitcoin or U.S. equity indexes actually rose or fell during those releases.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Editorial rule:</strong> We describe verified events and conditional economic mechanisms—not measured asset returns, a trading recommendation or the original USD Impact Score.</p></div>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="timeline"><div class="section-kicker">02 / Verified chronology</div><h2 id="timeline">What happened, in order</h2>
        <div class="timeline">
          <div class="event" data-claim="fomc-preview-oct26"><time datetime="2026-10-26">October 26–27 · Before the decision</time><h3>The Fed meeting was scheduled, not decided</h3><p>Early-week Daily editions documented the October 27–28 FOMC meeting on the official calendar. A calendar entry should never be counted as a policy outcome.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/2026-october.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Federal Reserve Board event calendar</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="fomc-outcome-oct28"><time datetime="2026-10-28">October 28 · Policy decision</time><h3>The target range stayed unchanged</h3><p>The FOMC maintained the federal funds target range at <strong>3.75%–4.00%</strong>. The statement recorded a <strong>10–2</strong> vote, with two voting dissenters, Lisa D. Cook and Beth M. Hammack, preferring a 25-basis-point increase. That describes the decision; it does not demonstrate a contemporaneous dollar-price reaction.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261028a.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official FOMC statement · 2:00 p.m. EDT issuer-stated release time</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="gdp-oct29"><time datetime="2026-10-29">October 29 · Economic growth</time><h3>Q3 real GDP advanced at a 3.4% annualized pace</h3><p>BEA’s advance estimate reported <strong>3.4%</strong> seasonally adjusted annualized real GDP growth in the third quarter, following <strong>2.2%</strong> in the second. Quarterly annualized growth is not the same measurement as year-over-year growth.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/gdp-advance-estimate-3rd-quarter-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BEA GDP advance release · 8:30 a.m. EDT issuer-stated release time</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="pce-oct29"><time datetime="2026-10-29">October 29 · Inflation</time><h3>Headline and core PCE remained distinct</h3><p>The September release reported <strong>3.6%</strong> headline PCE inflation and <strong>3.1%</strong> core PCE inflation over twelve months. These are year-over-year rates, not monthly changes or direct measurements of market expectations.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-september-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BEA Personal Income and Outlays release · 8:30 a.m. EDT</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="eci-oct30"><time datetime="2026-10-30">October 30 · Employment costs</time><h3>Employment costs rose 0.8% during Q3</h3><p>BLS reported a <strong>0.8%</strong> seasonally adjusted quarter-over-quarter increase in civilian compensation, compared with <strong>0.9%</strong> in the preceding quarter. The separate twelve-month increase was <strong>3.4%</strong> on a not-seasonally-adjusted basis.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/eci_10302026.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BLS Employment Cost Index release · 8:30 a.m. Eastern</a></div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="transmission"><div class="section-kicker">03 / Transmission framework</div><h2 id="transmission">Why the releases matter for the dollar</h2><p>Official facts are inputs to analysis, not a ready-made currency signal. The economic transmission chain is conditional: information changes expectations; expectations can affect rates, risk appetite and relative asset returns; market prices then reflect many simultaneous influences.</p>
        <div class="mechanics"><div class="mechanic"><span class="n">01 / POLICY</span><strong>Expected short rates</strong><p>FOMC decisions can shift the perceived path of future interest rates.</p></div><div class="mechanic"><span class="n">02 / ACTIVITY</span><strong>Growth and demand</strong><p>GDP data can change assessments of economic resilience and demand.</p></div><div class="mechanic"><span class="n">03 / PRICES</span><strong>Inflation and labor costs</strong><p>PCE and ECI can shape the outlook for inflation persistence.</p></div></div>
        <p>None of these channels establishes the direction, magnitude or timing of an actual move in the dollar, oil, natural gas, gold, Bitcoin or U.S. indexes. A realized-move claim requires independently verified, rights-cleared market observations not included in this report.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="mistakes"><div class="section-kicker">04 / Interpretation</div><h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
        <p><strong>Do not double-count.</strong> The October 26–27 meeting previews and October 28 policy decision are different stages of one event.</p>
        <p><strong>Do not mix measurement units.</strong> GDP growth is presented as an annualized quarterly pace; PCE uses twelve-month inflation rates; ECI includes both quarterly seasonally adjusted and annual not-seasonally-adjusted readings.</p>
        <p><strong>Do not invent the market reaction.</strong> A dated release does not establish a surprise relative to forecasts, nor can it prove what any asset price did on that day.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="watch"><div class="section-kicker">05 / What to watch</div><h2 id="watch">Questions for the next release cycle</h2>
        <p>Track the next official calendar, distinguish scheduled announcements from published outcomes, and check the reference period and units of every observation. When new facts appear, review their potential policy implications before drawing conditional cross-asset connections.</p>
        <p>The method is straightforward: <strong>verify the source, date the evidence, distinguish mechanism from observed price movement, and record what remains unknown.</strong></p>
      </section>
